    "description": "Hansard of 2025-10-23",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.parliament.lk/en/business-of-parliament/hansards",
    "lang": "si-ta-en",
    "url_pdf": "https://www.parliament.lk/uploads/businessdocs/english/22641_english_2025-10-23.pdf",
    "chunk_id": "2025-10-23-2025-10-23-0058",
    "chunk_index": 58,
    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "4cdb692cfc22f0eb6bfd46dbdc7fedd2",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2053,
    "chunk_text": "had been affected. However, since they reverted to manual processing, no service delivery had been affected during the collapse which prolonged for one week. Therefore, we have not received any information about any financial or economic impact to any of these institutions up to this moment.\n\ni. Implementation of the National Cyber\n\nSecurity Policy\n\nThis will have all the systems assessed through a Vulnerability Assessment and Penetration Test and the respective institutions informed to rectify them.\n\nii. Critical Information Infrastructure - CII\n\nWe have identified Critical Information\n\n2529 2530\n\n[\u0dbb\u0dbb\u0dd4 \u0d91\u0dbb\u0dc0\u0dbb \u0dc0\u0dd3\u0dbb\u0dbb\u0dad\u0dca\u0daf\u0dca\u200d\u0db1 \u0db8\u0dc4\u0dad\u0ddc]\n\n2025 \u0d94\u0d9a\u0dca\u0db1\u0dad\u0dba\u0db6\u0dbb\u0dca 23\n\nInfrastructure across key sectors such as finance, health, energy, public administration and so on to ensure that we give focus to those systems to prevent any kind of cyber security attacks and to be resilient on them.\n\niii. Establishment of the National Cyber Security\n\nOperations Centre - NCSOC\n\nWe have established a month ago - in September - the National Cyber Security Operation Centre. This will centrally monitor all these Critical Information Infrastructure - CII - 24/7 and that monitoring will ensure that there will be no external cyber attacks on those systems. This centralized monitoring will also enable us to respond to any such breach attempts much proactively and in real time.\n\niv. AI-powered Threat Detection at NCSOC\n\nWe are going to enhance the NCSOC with AI-powered threat detection along with malware and threat-hunting software to enable predictive detection of evolving cyber threats and early prevention of capabilities to safeguard national digital infrastructure.\n\nv. Drafting of a New Cyber Security Law\n\nAlso, we are drafting a new cyber security law to establish Cyber Security Regulatory Authority, which will be empowered both with regulatory oversight and enforcement authority across critical public and private infrastructure to ensure that those infrastructure will uphold all the security standards."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_hansard_2020s",
    "doc_id": "2025-10-23-2025-10-23",
    "num": "2025-10-23",
    "date_str": "2025-10-23",
    "description": "Hansard of 2025-10-23",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.parliament.lk/en/business-of-parliament/hansards",
    "lang": "si-ta-en",
    "url_pdf": "https://www.parliament.lk/uploads/businessdocs/english/22641_english_2025-10-23.pdf",
    "chunk_id": "2025-10-23-2025-10-23-0059",
    "chunk_index": 59,
    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "92c983110f32a92d4734ef6822095b71",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1964,
    "chunk_text": "Also, we are drafting a new cyber security law to establish Cyber Security Regulatory Authority, which will be empowered both with regulatory oversight and enforcement authority across critical public and private infrastructure to ensure that those infrastructure will uphold all the security standards.\n\nThen, we are going to become a signatory to the United Nations Convention against Cybercrime to strengthen cross-border collaboration on cybercrime investigations and to secure evidence sharing and legal cooperation. This is a strategic step to bolster global trust in the security of Sri Lanka\u2019s e-government systems.\n\nvii. Joint Cybersecurity Task Force for Financial Sector Protection\n\nA collaborative mechanism has been initiated by the Ministry of Digital Economy, Sri Lanka CERT, and TRCSL in partnership with CBSL and all licensed banks to proactively counter cyber threats-particularly phishing and digital fraud targeting the financial systems.\n\n8. Yes, the Cabinet process to initiate a comprehensive Cybersecurity Law is currently underway to address this critical requirement. A Cybersecurity Regulatory Authority will be established, as I mentioned before, to critically monitor all the critical digital infrastructure of the Government.\n\nAlso, we have established the National Cybersecurity Operations Centre to monitor 37 key government institutions initially and currently, four institutions have already been connected and another six are in the process of integration.\n\n9. In response to Question Nos. 7 and 8, I provided details about the cybersecurity measures that have been taken. Other than that, I want to highlight the fact that the Digital Economy Blueprint outlines Sri Lanka\u2019s strategic road map for digital transformation. It is anchored in a layered architecture comprising core digital infrastructure, Digital Public Infrastructure and a suite of platforms and applications which deliver services to citizens and businesses."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_hansard_2020s",
    "doc_id": "2025-10-23-2025-10-23",
    "num": "2025-10-23",
    "date_str": "2025-10-23",
    "description": "Hansard of 2025-10-23",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.parliament.lk/en/business-of-parliament/hansards",
    "lang": "si-ta-en",
    "url_pdf": "https://www.parliament.lk/uploads/businessdocs/english/22641_english_2025-10-23.pdf",
    "chunk_id": "2025-10-23-2025-10-23-0060",
    "chunk_index": 60,
    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "f514e7b7863714c689f5cd86bfa6c2a5",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1767,
    "chunk_text": "9. In response to Question Nos. 7 and 8, I provided details about the cybersecurity measures that have been taken. Other than that, I want to highlight the fact that the Digital Economy Blueprint outlines Sri Lanka\u2019s strategic road map for digital transformation. It is anchored in a layered architecture comprising core digital infrastructure, Digital Public Infrastructure and a suite of platforms and applications which deliver services to citizens and businesses.\n\nAnd also, the Cabinet has directed the initiation of legislative processes to establish two critical institutions. One is the Digital Economy Authority - DEA - to drive and govern the digital economy agenda. The other one is the Cybersecurity Regulatory Authority - CSRA - to enforce the implementation of security in all the Government digital platforms.\n\n10. The Government is taking comprehensive steps to ensure that such a disruption does not recur, strengthening both the technical infrastructure and the governance systems that underpin national digital services. The ICTA is currently in the process of migrating workloads to the new LGC 2.5 from the Legacy system so that we will not have reliance on the old system. The new one will have geo-redundant system architecture, centralized backup systems and so on.\n\nThe disk utilization was brought down to 88 per cent or below and also, as and when the workloads are migrated to the new LGC, those will be removed from the current Legacy to free more space. And the National Cybersecurity Operations Centre - NCSOC - will monitor the LGC 2.5, 24/7.\n\nThe SLT Data Centre - NOC, Network Operations Centre - will monitor the new system 24/7 to ensure that it maintains all the KPIs round the clock, ensuring service integrity and performance."
  },
