    "md5": "69ad95d09f7dd627840b3391eb1c1054",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2062,
    "chunk_text": "The FTA\u2019s core outcomes are clear: thousands of tariff lines are now duty-free, with additional quotas for key products such as tea and garments. Bilateral trade multiplied in the 2000s and the Agreement gave many Sri Lankan firms their first real foothold in a vast neighbouring market. But, the structure is now outdated and there are non-tariff frictions - standards, testing, logistics, rules-of-origin compliance - which often blunt the headline preferences. An upgrade is overdue.\n\n\u0dc0\u0d9c\u0dad\u0dd9\u0db1\u0db1 \u0daf\u0dc4\u0dc2\u0dba\u0dc0\u0db4\u0db1\u0dc3\u0db1\u0dba 6t) \u0dd4\u200d\u0db4\u0dca\u0d9a\u0dca\u0dc2\u0dd2\u0dad \u0daf\u0dc4\u0dc2\u0dba\u0dc0\u0db4\u0db1\u0dc3\u0db1\u200d\u0d86 \u0dc0\u0dd0\u0dd2\u0dc0\u0dd3\u0dd9 6t) \u0dd4\u0d9a \u200d\u0db6\u0ddd\u0d9c\u0dba\n\n2024 2025 2026 2027\n\n\u0dd9\u0da4\u0dc0\u200d\u0da4\u0db1\u0d9a\u0dca\u0dc2 \u0db1 356,125 5,000 8,000 10,000\n\n\u0dc0\u0dd0\u0dbd\u0dca \u0dd4\u0d9a\n\n\u0dc3\u0dd0\u0db1\u0da7 \u0daf\u0dc4\u0dc2\u0dba\u0dc0\u0db4\u0db1\u0dc3\u0db1\n\n\u0dc3\u0dad\u0dca\u0dad \u200d\u0db1\u0db1\u0dd9\u0dd0\u0dad.**\n\n1,500 3,000 5,000\n\n\u0d9a\u0dd2\u0dbb\u0dd2 \u0dd4\u0d9a 1,000 2,000 5,000 \u0d89\u0db1\u0dca\u0daf\u0db1\u0d9a 1,000 2,000 5,000\n\nThat upgrade is the proposed Economic and Technology Cooperation Agreement, ETCA, a modern framework to extend cooperation beyond goods into services, investments, technology and mutual recognition of standards and qualifications. Talks restarted in November, 2023 after years of pause, but have since drifted. As of late, in September, 2025, India had asked Sri Lanka to appoint a chief negotiator to resume structured rounds of talks; we have not done so to date.\n\nMeanwhile, our trade gap with India remains widened. In 2024, Sri Lanka's exports to India were US Dollars 884 million and imported goods worth US Dollars 3.76 billion, an exports-to-imports ratio of roughly 24 per cent.\n\nSir, when I was Minister of Trade, that particular ratio was in the range of about 70 per cent to the advantage of India and at that time, it was improved to the benefit of Sri Lanka, but still, there is a huge gap that needs to be rectified or corrected to the advantage of Sri Lanka.\n\nWe should be using an upgraded pact to push more value-added exports, have easier access to services and investment in supply chains that create jobs at home."
