    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "cee322ef8fe66f2f31b1e24b88770dab",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1562,
    "chunk_text": "2. Non-Tariff Barriers What steps has the Government taken to address the non-tariff barriers and technical restrictions such as product testing, standards certification and Customs delays that continue to obstruct Sri Lankan exporters from fully utilizing the duty-free access granted under ISFTA? Sir, you could see that owing to imposition of tariff by President Trump, there is a huge potential that exists for us to export our textiles to India because the Indian middle-class population has gone up to about 400 or 500 million. So, there is a huge potential to take advantage of in the short term.\n\n259 260\n\n[\u0d9c\u0dbb\u0dd4 \u0db1\u0db1\u0dd9\u0dbd\u0dca \u0dbb\u0dd4\u0dab\u0db1\u0dbb\u0dad\u0dca\u0db1 \u0dd9\u0dca\u0dad\u0db1]\n\n2025 \u200d\u0db1\u0db1\u0dc0\u0dd0\u0db8\u0dca\u0db6\u0d9c 08\n\nperformance, covering sectoral winners and losers, net job creation, FDI flows and trade imbalances before proceeding with ECTA negotiations so that Parliament and the public are properly informed?\n\n9. Tourism, Ports and Banking Synergies\n\nIndia is today Sri Lanka\u2019s largest source of tourists, a key partner in Colombo and Trincomalee port activities and a growing force in cross-border banking and digital payments. What specific provisions would ETCA include to deepen cooperation in tourism promotion, port development and financial-sector integration so that these critical areas translate into higher earnings and job creation for Sri Lanka? Sir, I look forward to an Answer, especially after the President\u2019s Budget Speech yesterday, which impacts on neighbourly development. So, this is the way forward to lead with immediate effect."
  },
