    "chunk_text": "So, if not for that, we would have been in dire straits. But, all these things happened before this Government took over. And, now, the Government is continuing to abide by the rules set by the IMF, very obediently following their dictates.\n\nSpeaking about the CEB, I would like to move on to another important factor. Now, in the public domain, there is a discussion regarding an issue relating to the grand theft of our nation\u2019s power. It is arguably the most brazen, calculated and unconscionable raid on the public purse in the history of this Republic. What we are witnessing, Sir, is not a mere accounting anomaly. I am talking about the grand theft, an orchestrated plunder of the Ceylon Electricity Board executed over decades by a cabal of 15 individuals led by one U. D. Jayawardena, who weaponized insider access and fiduciary trust to siphon billions from the State. This is not conjecture, Sir; on those findings, discussions are going on in this Parliament now and this is in the public domain.\n\nNow, Sir, let me talk about the anatomy of this theft. In 1980, the CEB founded the Lanka Transformers Limited to serve public interest. It was a State-owned entity with 70 per cent of its shares held by the CEB. Over time, foreign shareholders wanted to exist from the company. Then, what happened? Instead of the CEB reclaiming its rightful stake, the Board, chaired by Mr. Nalinda Illangakoon, declined to purchase the shares, citing cash flow constraints. It was worth a mere Rs. 500 million. The CEB said, \u201cWe cannot pay this money. We have cash flow constraints.\u201d Then, what happened? Then came the betrayal. Mr. Illangakoon, wearing two hats as the Chairman of both the CEB and the LTL, facilitated the transfer of those shares to a private trust - LTG ESOT - controlled by the very insiders who orchestrated the scheme. That trust, now renamed \u201cPeradev (Pvt.) Limited\u201d, is 82 per cent owned by that robber gang of 15 engineers. They, now, beneficially own almost 30 per\n\n401 402"
