    "md5": "90a58f61024f01a83a72f2f49ddc5480",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1372,
    "chunk_text": "Now, Sir, let me talk about the anatomy of this theft. In 1980, the CEB founded the Lanka Transformers Limited to serve public interest. It was a State-owned entity with 70 per cent of its shares held by the CEB. Over time, foreign shareholders wanted to exist from the company. Then, what happened? Instead of the CEB reclaiming its rightful stake, the Board, chaired by Mr. Nalinda Illangakoon, declined to purchase the shares, citing cash flow constraints. It was worth a mere Rs. 500 million. The CEB said, \u201cWe cannot pay this money. We have cash flow constraints.\u201d Then, what happened? Then came the betrayal. Mr. Illangakoon, wearing two hats as the Chairman of both the CEB and the LTL, facilitated the transfer of those shares to a private trust - LTG ESOT - controlled by the very insiders who orchestrated the scheme. That trust, now renamed \u201cPeradev (Pvt.) Limited\u201d, is 82 per cent owned by that robber gang of 15 engineers. They, now, beneficially own almost 30 per\n\n401 402\n\ncent of LTL Holdings without investing a single rupee! How is this happening? This is a very serious matter, Sir. The value of this almost 30 per cent stake is estimated to be between Rs. 34.4 billion and Rs. 64.5 billion, as per independent valuation done by the NDB Investment Bank and the CT CLSA Capital (Pvt.) Limited. Sir, I table* a few documents related to this issue."
  },
