    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "303177f8a94017ba823f02d9d8ae48b2",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1844,
    "chunk_text": "If so, what remedial or legislative measures have been taken to regularize the decision, restore Sri Lanka\u2019s sovereign credit rating and ensure that future suspensions of debt obligations are subject to Parliamentary approval?\n\nSpecifically, does the Government intend to introduce a Sovereign Debt Management and Accountability Act to codify procedures and prevent unilateral Executive action in external debt matters?\n\n8. IMF Agreement and Citizen-centric Reforms\n\nPresident Ranil Wickremesinghe\u2019s decision to sign the Extended Fund Facility - EFF - Agreement with the International Monetary Fund - IMF- on the 28th of April, 2023, which was subsequently debated in this House, helped stabilize the economy and restore partial international confidence. While the present Government has also accepted the Agreement, could it now be made more citizen-friendly? What specific policy recalibrations or amendments have been proposed to ensure equitable burden-sharing, social protection and transparency in its implementation?\n\nSir, I am asking for reasons because the IMF is only interested in fiscal maintenance and the Central Bank is only interested in looking at the interest rates and the exchange rates. But, Parliament is held responsible or accountable for restoring the growth of the country.\n\nWhen we asked the Central Bank Governor about that at the Committee on Public Finance last week, his answer was, I quote, \u201cYou can ask the Finance Minister\u201d. And, that is the reason why I am asking it now.\n\n9. Conditions for Credit Restoration\n\nWhat specific conditions, structural benchmarks or policy reforms have been imposed by international lenders and credit-rating agencies for reinstating Sri Lanka\u2019s normal access to concessional and commercial borrowing?\n\n10. Quantification of Economic, Fiscal, Human and Reputational Cost"
